{"outcome":"built","summary":"I built a 3-cell organizer with 29 parts. There are two full hex bases, each holding a removable 3-compartment bin. A north-half base holds a drawer, which opens from its cut face. One bin cell has a top cover with a carry handle on it. I capped the open faces and bolted the joints together. check_build found no rule issues and the mesh check came back clear.

The bins are dressed with props: M3 screws, nuts and standoffs. The two props in the bin under the handle cover can't be seen until you lift the cover. The drawer is empty because the small-parts heaps wouldn't fit in it. I took out a cover I'd added over the drawer cell to bring the count from 32 down to 29 parts, under my 30-part limit for "portable".

Whether the handle and joints are strong enough to carry the loaded organizer depends on the material and how it's printed. The configurator can't check that.","requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"A base for an electronics organizer, compact enough to be portable","status":"met","evidence":"The base is three cells (piece:0,0,0, piece:1,0,0, piece:0,1,0) with 29 parts, under the 30-part limit I set. The size is checked, but the configurator can't check weight or strength."},{"id":"R2","requirement":"Removable bins for small parts","status":"met","evidence":"Two drop-in hex-bin-full-div3 bins (bin:0,0,0 and bin:1,0,0) hold M3 screws, nuts and standoffs. The bin at 0,0,0 sits under the handle cover, so you lift the cover to get at it."},{"id":"R3","requirement":"At least one drawer","status":"met","evidence":"drawer:0#0,1#cut on the half base at 0,1."},{"id":"R4","requirement":"A carry handle","status":"met","evidence":"handle:0,0,0;top sits on the top cover of cell 0,0,0. The configurator can't check whether it can take the load."}]}
